// Extensively maps all Historiography, Prehistoric, Ancient, Medieval, Modern, World History, and Art & Culture topics.

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA = window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA || {};
window.EXPERT_REVISION_DATA = window.EXPERT_REVISION_DATA || {};

// ==========================================
// 1. HISTORIOGRAPHY & HISTORICAL FOUNDATIONS
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["what-is-history"] = `
  <h2>📚 Meaning and Scope of History</h2>
  <p>History is the systematic analysis of past human events, cultures, and structures. It is not a static list of dates, but a dynamic, interpretative science that examines cause-and-effect relationships over time.</p>
  
  <h3>1. Core Foundations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Definition:</strong> The word comes from Greek <em>Historia</em> (inquiry/knowledge acquired by investigation). E.H. Carr defined it as "an unending dialogue between the present and the past."</li>
    <li><strong>Importance of Chronology:</strong> Establishes the exact sequence of events, helping to avoid anachronisms and trace the evolutionary progression of human society.</li>
    <li><strong>Cause and Effect:</strong> Every historical event has roots in economic, social, political, or geographical causes, and produces consequences that shape subsequent eras.</li>
    <li><strong>Periodisation of Indian History:</strong> Originally divided by colonial historians (like James Mill) into Hindu, Muslim, and British periods (a communal division). Modern historians classify it scientifically into <strong>Ancient, Medieval, and Modern</strong> periods.</li>
    <li><strong>Social, Economic & Cultural History:</strong> Shift from dynastic history (kings and wars) to social structures (caste, gender), economic patterns (trade routes, land revenue), and cultural expressions (art, religion).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["sources-indian-history"] = `
  <h2>🏛️ Sources of Indian History</h2>
  <p>Historians reconstruct the past using two primary categories of sources: Archaeological and Literary.</p>

  <h3>1. Archaeological Sources (High-Yield)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Inscriptions (Epigraphy):</strong> The most reliable source.
      <ul>
        <li><em>Ashokan Edicts:</em> Written in Brahmi, Kharosthi, Greek, and Aramaic scripts. Deciphered by **James Prinsep** in 1837.</li>
        <li><em>Allahabad Pillar Inscription (Prasasti):</em> Composed in Sanskrit by Harishena, praising Gupta King Samudragupta.</li>
        <li><em>Aihole Inscription:</em> Written by Ravikirti, describing Chalukya King Pulakeshin II's victory over Harsha.</li>
        <li><em>Uttaramerur Inscription:</em> Details the self-governing village administration of the Imperial Cholas.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Coins (Numismatics):</strong> Tell us about economic health, trade relations, and kingly interests.
      <ul>
        <li>Punch-marked coins (silver/copper) were the earliest.</li>
        <li>**Indo-Greeks** were the first to issue coins with portraits and legends.</li>
        <li>**Kushanas** issued the first large-scale gold coins in India.</li>
        <li>**Guptas** issued the largest number of gold coins (Dinars). Samudragupta's coins show him playing the Veena.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Monuments & Pottery:</strong> Cave temples (Ajanta/Ellora), stupas (Sanchi). Pottery sequences like **Ochre Coloured Pottery (OCP)**, **Painted Grey Ware (PGW - Iron Age)**, and **Northern Black Polished Ware (NBPW - Mauryan)** act as chronological markers.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Literary Sources</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Religious Texts:</strong> Vedas, Puranas, Epics (Ramayana, Mahabharata), Buddhist Tripitakas (Pali), and Jain Angas (Prakrit).</li>
    <li><strong>Secular Literature:</strong> 
      <ul>
        <li>Kautilya's <em>Arthashastra</em> (statecraft and administration).</li>
        <li>Panini's <em>Ashtadhyayi</em> (earliest Sanskrit grammar).</li>
        <li>Kalhana's <em>Rajatarangini</em> (12th century, history of Kashmir, India's first scientific historical work).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Foreign Accounts:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Greek:** Megasthenes (<em>Indica</em>, Mauryan administration).</li>
        <li>**Chinese:** Fa-Hien (Gupta period under Chandragupta II) and Hiuen Tsang (Harsha's reign, studied at Nalanda).</li>
        <li>**Arab/Persian:** Al-Biruni (<em>Kitab-ul-Hind</em>, 11th century culture and science), Ibn Battuta (<em>Rihla</em>, Tughlaq period).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["dating-systems"] = `
  <h2>📅 Dating Systems and Eras</h2>
  <p>Different calendars and dating conventions have been used throughout Indian history to record time.</p>

  <h3>1. General Terminology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BC (Before Christ) / BCE (Before Common Era):</strong> Counted backward. Year 500 BCE is older than 300 BCE.</li>
    <li><strong>AD (Anno Domini) / CE (Common Era):</strong> Counted forward from the birth of Christ.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Key Historical Eras of India</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
