      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Founder / Context</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Significance</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Vikrama Era</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">57 BCE</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">King Vikramaditya of Ujjain</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Commemorated victory over Saka invaders.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Saka Era</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">78 CE</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kushana King Kanishka</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Adopted as the **National Calendar of India** in 1957. Starts on Chaitra 1 (March 22 / 21 in leap years).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Gupta Era</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">319 - 320 CE</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Chandragupta I</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Marks his accession to the throne and rise of Gupta power.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Harsha Era</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">606 CE</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Harshavardhana of Kanauj</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Commemorated his coronation.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>
`;

// ==========================================
// 2. PREHISTORIC INDIA
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["stone-age"] = `
  <h2>🗿 The Stone Age</h2>
  <p>Prehistoric India is divided into Paleolithic, Mesolithic, and Neolithic ages based on stone tool technology and lifestyle shifts.</p>

  <h3>1. Paleolithic (Old Stone Age): Up to 10,000 BCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lifestyle:</strong> Nomadic hunter-gatherers. Lived in caves and rock shelters. Fire was discovered but not widely controlled for cooking.</li>
    <li><strong>Tools:</strong> Large, unpolished stone tools made of **quartzite** (hence Paleolithic humans are called "Quartzite Men"). Hand-axes, cleavers, choppers.</li>
    <li><strong>Phases:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li><em>Lower Paleolithic:</em> Hand-axes, cleavers. Sites: Soan Valley (Punjab, Pakistan), Bori (Maharashtra).</li>
        <li><em>Middle Paleolithic:</em> Flake tools. Sites: Narmada Valley, Nevasa (Maharashtra).</li>
        <li><em>Upper Paleolithic:</em> Bone tools, blades. Coincided with modern humans (Homo sapiens). Sites: Kurnool caves (AP - evidence of ash).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Mesolithic (Middle Stone Age): 10,000 BCE - 6,000 BCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Climatic Shift:</strong> Transition to a warmer, drier climate, leading to changes in flora and fauna.</li>
    <li><strong>Tools:</strong> **Microliths** - extremely small, pointed stone tools (1-5 cm long) fitted onto wooden or bone handles (spears, arrows).</li>
    <li><strong>Lifestyle:</strong> Hunting-gathering transitioned to early animal domestication. Dog was the first animal domesticated.</li>
    <li><strong>Key Sites:</strong> **Bagor** (Rajasthan - earliest evidence of domestication), **Adamgarh** (MP), Langhnaj (Gujarat).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Neolithic (New Stone Age): 6,000 BCE - 1,000 BCE</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Neolithic Revolution:</strong> Transition from food-gathering to **food-producing** (agriculture).</li>
    <li><strong>Lifestyle:</strong> Settled village life. Invention of the wheel, cultivation of wheat, barley, ragi, and rice. Domestication of sheep, goats, cattle. Production of hand-made and wheel-made pottery.</li>
    <li><strong>Key Sites:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Mehrgarh (Balochistan, Pakistan):** Earliest Neolithic site (~7000 BCE). Evidence of wheat/barley farming and mud-brick houses.</li>
        <li>**Burzahom (Kashmir):** Unique pit-dwellings (caves dug in ground to escape cold) and practice of burying dogs with masters.</li>
        <li>**Koldihwa (UP):** Earliest evidence of **rice cultivation** globally (~6000 BCE).</li>
        <li>**Chirand (Bihar):** Large number of bone tools made of deer antlers.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["chalcolithic-age"] = `\n
  <h2>⛏️ The Chalcolithic & Pottery Cultures</h2>
  <p>The transition period from the Stone Age to the Metal Age, characterized by the joint use of Copper and Stone.</p>

  <h3>1. Prehistoric Pottery Cultures Timeline</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Culture</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Period</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Pottery Types</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Mehrgarh Culture</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">7000-2600 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Red Ware, Black-on-Red Ware</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Indus Valley Civilization</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">2600-1900 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Painted Grey Ware, Black Polished Ware</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ochre Coloured Pottery Culture</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">2000-1500 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ochre Coloured Pottery (OCP)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Malwa Culture</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">1500-500 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Malwa Ware (Red and black pottery)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Early Iron Age Cultures</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">1200-600 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Northern Black Polished Ware, Painted Grey Ware (PGW)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Megalithic Cultures</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">1000-300 BCE</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Black and Red Ware</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>2. Major Chalcolithic Cultures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Ahar-Banas Culture (Rajasthan: 2500-1500 BCE):</strong> Sites like Ahar and Gilund. Houses made of stone and mud. Copper smelting furnaces found.</li>
    <li><strong>Jorwe Culture (Maharashtra: 1400-700 BCE):</strong> Most extensive. Sites: Inamgaon and Daimabad. Highly settled, fortified villages.</li>
  </ul>
\n`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["rock-art"] = `
  <h2>🎨 Prehistoric Rock Art</h2>
  <p>Prehistoric rock art provides invaluable insights into the daily life, beliefs, and cognitive evolution of early humans.</p>

  <h3>1. Bhimbetka Caves (Madhya Pradesh)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Discovery:</strong> Discovered by archaeologist **V.S. Wakankar** in 1957. Located in Vindhya Range, MP.</li>
    <li><strong>UNESCO Status:</strong> Declared a UNESCO World Heritage site in 2003.</li>
    <li><strong>Artistic Styles:</strong> Displays continuous artistic activity from the Upper Paleolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic, Chalcolithic, to Historical periods.</li>
    <li><strong>Themes & Pigments:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Mainly depicts wild animals (bison, tigers, elephants), hunting scenes, community dances, battles, and family life.</li>
        <li>**Colors:** Used mineral colors mixed with water/animal fat. **Red** (from hematite/geru for hunters) and **Green** (from chalcedony for dancers) are the most prominent.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

// ==========================================
// 3. ANCIENT INDIA
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["indus-valley-civilization"] = `
  <h2>🌊 Indus Valley Civilization (IVC)</h2>
  <p>Also known as the Harappan Civilization. India's first urban revolution, flourishing in the Bronze Age (~2500 BCE - 1900 BCE).</p>

  <h3>1. Discovery & Chronology</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>First discovered in 1921 at **Harappa** by **Daya Ram Sahni** under John Marshall.</li>
    <li>**Mohenjo-daro** discovered in 1922 by **R.D. Banerji**.</li>
    <li>Dated via Carbon-14. Mature Phase: 2600 - 1900 BCE.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Town Planning & Drainage (Exhaustively Tested)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Grid System:</strong> Streets cut each other at right angles, dividing the city into rectangular blocks.</li>
    <li><strong>Citadel & Lower Town:</strong> Most cities were divided into a raised Citadel (west, housing public buildings, elites) and a Lower Town (east, housing commoners). Exception: **Dholavira** had three divisions (Citadel, Middle, Lower).</li>
    <li><strong>Drainage System:</strong> Advanced underground covered drains made of burnt bricks, with manholes/soak pits at regular intervals. House drains connected directly to main street drains.</li>
    <li><strong>Building Material:</strong> Standardized burnt bricks with a ratio of **4:2:1** (length:breadth:thickness).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Site-Wise High-Yield Facts</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Site</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Location & River</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Key Discoveries & Signature Features</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Harappa</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Punjab (Pak), Ravi</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">6 Granaries in a row, stone symbols of Lingam and Yoni, coffin burials (R-37 cemetery).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Mohenjo-daro</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Sindh (Pak), Indus</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Meaning: "Mound of the Dead". **Great Bath**, Great Granary, Bronze Dancing Girl, Pashupati Seal, Bearded Priest.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Lothal</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Gujarat, Bhogava</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">**Artificial Dockyard** (trade with Mesopotamia), double burials (male/female together), rice husk.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Kalibangan</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Rajasthan, Ghaggar</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Meaning: "Black Bangles". **Ploughed field surface** (earliest evidence), fire altars, camel bones.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Dholavira</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Gujarat (Kutch)</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Tripartite division, massive **water reservoirs**, stadium, billboard/signboard containing 10 large Harappan signs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Chanhudaro</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Sindh (Pak), Indus</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Only city **without a Citadel**. Bead-making factory, inkwell, lipstick imprint on brick.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Surkotada</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Gujarat</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Only site containing actual **horse bones**. Pot burials.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>4. Economy, Script & Decline</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Economy:</strong> Agriculture-based (wheat, barley, cotton - first to produce cotton globally, called <em>Sindon</em> by Greeks). Trade with Mesopotamia (called <em>Meluhha</em> in cuneiform texts).</li>
    <li><strong>Script & Seals:</strong> Pictographic script (undeciphered). Written in **Boustrophedon** style (right-to-left, then left-to-right). Seals mostly made of **steatite** (soapstone), rectangular/square showing animals.</li>
    <li><strong>Decline Theories:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>Aryan Invasion: Mortimer Wheeler (criticised now).</li>
        <li>Floods: Marshall, S.R. Rao.</li>
        <li>Ecological Imbalance / Drying of Rivers: Sood and Agrawal, Rafique Mughal.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["vedic-age"] = `
  <h2>🌾 The Vedic Age</h2>
  <p>Reconstructed using the Vedas. Divided into Early Vedic (1500-1000 BCE) and Later Vedic (1000-600 BCE) periods.</p>

  <h3>1. Early Vedic Period (1500 - 1000 BCE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Geography:</strong> Settled in the **Sapta-Sindhu** region (Land of 7 Rivers: Indus, Saraswati, and 5 rivers of Punjab).</li>
    <li><strong>Polity:</strong> Tribal structure. King (Rajan) was not absolute, advised by assemblies: **Sabha** (council of elders), **Samiti** (general tribal assembly), and **Vidatha** (oldest folk assembly). Women attended Sabha/Vidatha.</li>
    <li><strong>Society & Economy:</strong> Pastoral economy. Cattle (Gau) was the main form of wealth (wars fought for cows called <em>Gavisthi</em>). Society was egalitarian; caste system (Varna) was flexible, based on occupation, not birth.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Later Vedic Period (1000 - 600 BCE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Geography:</strong> Shifted eastward to the Gangetic Valley (Aryavarta). Use of iron (called <em>Shyama Ayas</em>) cleared thick forests.</li>
    <li><strong>Polity:</strong> Territorial kingdoms (Janapadas) emerged. Royal power increased. Sabha and Samiti lost power; Vidatha was abolished. Women were no longer allowed to attend assemblies.</li>
    <li><strong>Society & Economy:</strong> Transition to an agrarian economy. Caste system became rigid and hereditary, split into 4 Varnas (Brahmins, Kshatriyas, Vaishyas, Shudras). 4 Ashramas (stages of life) emerged.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Vedic Literature</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Four Vedas:</strong> Rigveda (hymns), Samaveda (music/chants), Yajurveda (rituals), Atharvaveda (magic/spells).</li>
    <li><strong>Brahmanas:</strong> Prose texts explaining the rituals of the Vedas.</li>
    <li><strong>Aranyakas:</strong> Forest books dealing with mysticism and philosophy.</li>
    <li><strong>Upanishads:</strong> Philosophical texts (Vedanta). Total 108. **"Satyameva Jayate"** is in **Mundaka Upanishad**.</li>
  </ul>
`;

