    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kaushambi</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kausambi</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Allahabad, UP</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Udayana</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Yamuna</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kuru</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Hastinapura</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Haryana & Delhi</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Sudas</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Yamuna</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Magadha</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Rajagriha/Pataliputra</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Bihar</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Bimbisara, Ajatashatru</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ganga</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Matsya</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Virat Nagari</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Alwar, Rajasthan</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Unknown</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Chambal</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Panchala</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ahichatra/Kampilya</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Uttarakhand & UP</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Chetaka, Drupada</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ganga</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Surasena</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Mathura</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Uttar Pradesh</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kamsa</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Yamuna</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Vajjis/Vrijji</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Vaishali</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Bihar</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Confederacy</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ganga</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Malla</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kushinagar/Pava</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Deoria, UP</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Republic</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Ganga</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>2. Rise of Magadha</h3>
  <p>Magadha rose to become the supreme power due to: large iron deposits (for weapons/tools), strategic capitals (Rajgir surrounded by hills; Pataliputra at river junctions), and first-time use of elephants in wars.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Haryanka Dynasty (600 - 413 BCE):</strong> 
      <ul>
        <li>**Bimbisara:** Founder. Expanded via matrimonial alliances. First king to have a standing army.</li>
        <li>**Ajatashatru:** Killed his father Bimbisara. Conquered Vaishali using war engines *Mahasilakantaka* and *Rathamusala*. Patronised the **1st Buddhist Council** at Rajgir.</li>
        <li>**Udayin:** Shifted the capital from Rajgir to **Pataliputra**.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Shishunaga Dynasty (412 - 345 BCE):</strong> Founded by Shishunaga. Shifted capital from Pataliputra to Vaishali. Kalasoka (Kakarvarna) patronised the **2nd Buddhist Council**.</li>
    <li><strong>Nanda Dynasty (345 - 321 BCE):</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Mahapadma Nanda:** Took the title of *Ekarat*. First non-Kshatriya dynasty.</li>
        <li>**Dhana Nanda:** Last ruler. Alexander the Great invaded North-West India during his reign. Overthrown by Chandragupta Maurya.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
\n`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["buddhism-jainism"] = `\n
  <h2>🧘 Buddhism & Jainism</h2>
  <p>Heterodox religious movements that arose in the 6th century BCE as a reaction to Vedic ritualism and caste rigidity.</p>

  <h3>1. Buddhism (Gautama Buddha: 563 - 483 BCE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Born as Siddhartha in Lumbini (Nepal). Renounced home at 29. Attained Enlightenment under Bodhi tree in Bodh Gaya. First sermon at Sarnath (First Turning of the Wheel of Dharma). Died at Kushinagar.</li>
    <li><strong>Core Teachings:</strong> 4 Noble Truths, Eightfold Path, Middle Way. Three Jewels (Buddha, Dharma, Sangha).</li>
    <li><strong>Literature:</strong> Tripitaka (Pali Canon), Mahayana Sutras, Jataka Tales.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Buddhist Schisms & Sects</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Aspect</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Mahayana Buddhism (Great Vehicle)</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Hinayana/Theravada (Lesser Vehicle)</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Goal</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Buddhahood for all beings</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Personal enlightenment (Arhatship)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Bodhisattvas</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Highly revered, active role</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Less emphasis on their role</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Scriptures</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Expanded canon (Sanskrit)</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Pali Canon (Tripitaka)</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Idol Worship</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Yes, Buddha as a deity</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">No, Buddha as a teacher/guide</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Jainism (Vardhamana Mahavira: 599 - 527 BCE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>24th Tirthankara. Born at Kundagrama (Vaishali). Renunciation at 30. Attained Kevala Jnana (omniscience) after 12 years. Died at Pawapuri.</li>
    <li><strong>Core Teachings:</strong> Tri-ratnas (Right Faith, Right Knowledge, Right Conduct). 5 Vows (Ahimsa, Satya, Asteya, Aparigraha, Brahmacharya). **Anekantavada / Syadvada** (theory of multiple perspectives).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Jain Sects (Svetambar vs Digambar)</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Aspect</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Svetambar</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Digambar</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Clothing</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">White-clad monks/nuns</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Sky-clad (naked) monks</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Female Monks</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Allow ordination of women</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Do not believe women can attain Moksha directly</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Origin</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Teachings of Sthulabhadra</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Original teachings, led by Bhadrabahu</td></tr>
  </table>
\n`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["mauryan-period"] = `\n
  <h2>🛡️ The Mauryan Empire & Ashokan Edicts</h2>
  <p>India's first historical empire, establishing central control over most of the subcontinent.</p>

  <h3>1. Rulers & Triumphs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chandragupta Maurya (321 - 185 BCE):</strong> Overthrew Dhana Nanda with Chanakya. Defeated Selcus Nicator and married his daughter Helena. Adopted Jainism and committed Sallekhana.</li>
    <li><strong>Bindusara:</strong> Called *Amitrochates* (slayer of enemies). Joined Ajivika Sect.</li>
    <li><strong>Ashoka the Great:</strong> After Kalinga war, renounced violence. Spread Buddhism via missionaries and built stupas.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Comprehensive Ashokan Edicts</h3>
  <p>Ashoka's inscriptions on pillars and rocks provided insights into governance, policies, and religious tolerance.</p>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Edict</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Key Description & Points</th>
    </tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict I</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Prohibition of animal sacrifice, especially during festive seasons.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict II</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Medical treatment of humans/animals. Mentions Pandyas, Satyapuras, Keralaputras of South India.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict III</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Generosity to Brahmins. Yuktas, Pradeshikas, Rajukas ordered to spread Dhamma every 5 years.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict IV</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Dhammaghosha (sound of righteousness) over Bherighosha (sound of war).</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict V</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">About Dhammamahamatras and treating slaves right.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Major Rock Edict XIII</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Mentions victory over Kalinga, Ashoka's remorse, and interactions with Greek Kings.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Rummindei Inscription</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Mentions exemption from bali and reduced taxes for Lumbini (Buddha's birthplace).</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Allahabad (Schism) Edict</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Urges Sangha members to avoid causing divisions. Also contains Samudragupta's inscription.</td></tr>
    <tr><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kandahar Inscription</td><td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Famous bilingual edict in Greek and Aramaic.</td></tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Decline of the Mauryan Empire</h3>
  <p>Weak successors, regional revolts, economic drain from military campaigns, and foreign invasions by Greeks, Sakas, and Parthians destabilized the empire.</p>
\n`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["post-mauryan-india"] = `\n
  <h2>🏺 Post-Mauryan Dynasties & Foreign Invasions</h2>
  <p>After the Mauryan decline, Magadha saw the rise of smaller dynasties, while the North-West faced foreign invasions.</p>

  <h3>1. Indigenous Dynasties (185 BCE - 321 CE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Shunga Dynasty:</strong> Pushyamitra Shunga established the dynasty. Followed Brahminism. Vasumitra defeated Greek king Menander (conversation compiled in "Milindapanha").</li>
    <li><strong>Kanva Dynasty:</strong> Founded by Vasudeva Kanva.</li>
    <li><strong>Satavahanas:</strong> Founded by Simuka. Capital at Pratistanpura (later Amaravati). Gautamiputra Satakarni was a powerful ruler (Nashik inscription). Patrons of the Amaravati school of Art.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Central Asian Invasions & Impacts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Indo-Greeks:</strong> First to introduce **Gold coins** in India. Introduced Hellenistic art in the north-west.</li>
    <li><strong>Sakas / Scythians:</strong> Replaced Indo-Greeks. Ruled via Kshatrapas. **Rudradaman I** repaired the Sudarshan Lake (Junagarh inscription).</li>
    <li><strong>Parthians:</strong> Originally from Iran. Famous ruler: Gondophernes (St. Thomas visited India during his reign).</li>
    <li><strong>Kushans (Yuechis):</strong> Settled in lower Indus basin.
      <ul>
        <li>Kadphises I laid the foundation. Wima Kadphises issued large numbers of gold coins.</li>
        <li>**Kanishka:** Issued highest metallic purity gold coins. Capitals at Peshawar and Mathura. Controlled the Silk Route. Patronised Buddhism.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Cultural Impact of Central Asian Contacts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Introduced cavalry techniques: toe stirrups, reins, saddles.</li>
    <li>Introduced turbans, tunics, trousers, and heavy long coats/boots.</li>
    <li>**Satrap System:** Hereditary dual rule (father & son ruling jointly).</li>
    <li>Rise of **Gandhara Art** (Greco-Roman, spiritual Buddha with half-closed eyes) and **Mathura Art** (indigenous, red sandstone).</li>
  </ul>
\n`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["gupta-period"] = `
  <h2>👑 The Gupta Empire (Golden Age: 320 - 550 CE)</h2>
  <p>An era of political unification, economic prosperity, and unmatched achievements in science, literature, and art.</p>

  <div style="text-align:center; margin: 15px 0;">
    <img src="images/history_mauryan_gupta_map.png" alt="Mauryan vs Gupta Empire Territorial Extent" style="max-width:100%; border-radius:8px; box-shadow:0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:0.9em; color:#888; margin-top:5px;">Comparative Territorial Extent: Mauryan Empire vs Gupta Empire (Golden Age of Arts & Sciences).</p>
  </div>

  <h3>1. Key Rulers & Expansion</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chandragupta I (319 - 335 CE):</strong> First imperial ruler. Started the Gupta Era (319 CE). Married Licchavi princess Kumaradevi.</li>
    <li><strong>Samudragupta (335 - 380 CE):</strong> Called **"Napoleon of India"** by V.A. Smith due to his military campaigns. Military conquests recorded in the **Allahabad Pillar Inscription (Prayag Prasasti)** composed by his court poet **Harishena**. Performed Ashvamedha sacrifice. Issued gold coins depicting him playing the Veena.</li>
    <li><strong>Chandragupta II (Vikramaditya: 380 - 415 CE):</strong> Defeated Sakas, annexed Western India. Court adorned by **Navaratnas** (Nine Gems, including **Kalidasa** and **Varahamihira**). Chinese pilgrim **Fa-Hien** visited India during his reign.</li>
    <li><strong>Kumargupta:</strong> Founded the famous **Nalanda University**.</li>
    <li><strong>Skandagupta:</strong> Successfully repelled the invading **Hunas** (White Huns).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Science, Literature & Golden Age Debate</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Literature:</strong> Kalidasa (plays: *Abhijnanasakuntalam*, *Malavikagnimitram*; poems: *Meghaduta*, *Raghuvamsa*). Shudraka (*Mrichchakatikam*). Vishakhadatta (*Devichandraguptam*, *Mudrarakshasa*).</li>
    <li><strong>Science & Math:</strong> **Aryabhata** (wrote *Aryabhatiya*, calculated value of Pi, explained eclipses, proved Earth rotates on axis). **Varahamihira** (wrote *Brihat Samhita*, *Panchasiddhantika*). **Dhanvantari** (medicine).</li>
    <li><strong>The "Golden Age" Debate:</strong> While literature and science peaked, some historians argue that feudalism increased, foreign trade declined, and untouchability grew, questioning the "Golden" tag for the masses.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["south-indian-kingdoms"] = `
  <h2>⚓ South Indian Kingdoms</h2>
  <p>The history of South India is marked by the Sangam Age, followed by the Pallavas and Chalukyas, and later the mighty Cholas.</p>

  <h3>1. The Sangam Age (3rd BCE - 3rd CE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Assemblies (Sangams) of Tamil poets held under Pandyan patronage. Key literature: **Tolkappiyam** (grammar), epics **Silappatikaram** (Ilango Adigal) and **Manimekalai** (Shattanar).</li>
    <li>Three kingdoms: Cheras (emblem: Bow), Cholas (emblem: Tiger, capital: Puhar, King Karikala built Grand Anicut), Pandyas (emblem: Fish, capital: Madurai).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Pallavas & Chalukyas (6th - 8th CE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pallavas of Kanchi:</strong> Mahendravarman I, Narasimhavarman I (defeated Chalukya King Pulakeshin II, took title *Vatapikonda*). Built the rock-cut **Rathas of Mahabalipuram** (Shore Temple).</li>
    <li><strong>Chalukyas of Vatapi:</strong> Pulakeshin II. Defeated Harshavardhana on the banks of Narmada (recorded in Aihole inscription). Defeated by Narasimhavarman I.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Imperial Cholas (9th - 12th CE)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Founded by Vijayalaya. Capital at Thanjavur.</li>
    <li>**Rajaraja Chola I (985 - 1014 CE):** Built the grand **Brihadeshwara (Rajarajeshwara) Temple** at Thanjavur (tallest Vimana in India). Annexed northern Sri Lanka.</li>
    <li>**Rajendra Chola I (1014 - 1044 CE):** Annexed entire Sri Lanka. Marched up to Ganges, took title *Gangaikonda Chola* and founded capital *Gangaikondacholapuram*. Sent naval expeditions to Southeast Asia (Srivijaya Kingdom), dominating the Bay of Bengal (called the "Chola Lake").</li>
    <li>**Village Self-Government:** The **Uttaramerur Inscription** of Parantaka I (907-955 CE) details the working of local assemblies (*Ur* and *Sabha*). Members were elected by lottery (*Kudavolai* system) under strict eligibility rules.</li>
  </ul>
`;

// ==========================================
// 4. MEDIEVAL INDIA
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["early-medieval-india"] = `
  <h2>🛡️ Early Medieval India</h2>
  <p>An era of regional fragmentation marked by the Tripartite Struggle and the rise of Rajput kingdoms.</p>

  <h3>1. Tripartite Struggle (8th - 10th CE)</h3>
  <p>A century-long struggle between three major empires to control **Kannauj** (symbol of sovereignty in North India, situated in the fertile Gangetic plains):</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Palas of Bengal:</strong> Founded by Gopala. Dharmapala founded **Vikramashila University** and revived Nalanda. Followed Mahayana Buddhism.</li>
    <li><strong>The Gurjara-Pratiharas:</strong> Ruled Western India/Rajasthan. Mihira Bhoja was the greatest ruler.</li>
    <li><strong>The Rashtrakutas:</strong> Ruled Deccan. Founded by Dantidurga. **Krishna I** carved the monolithic **Kailash Temple at Ellora (Cave 16)**. Patronised Amoghavarsha (wrote *Kavirajamarga*).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["delhi-sultanate"] = `
  <h2>⚔️ The Delhi Sultanate (1206 - 1526)</h2>
  <p>Five successive dynasties ruled Delhi before the Mughal era. Mnemonic: <strong>S</strong>ome <strong>K</strong>ings <strong>T</strong>ravel <strong>S</strong>lowly <strong>L</strong>ately.</p>

  <h3>1. The Dynasties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Slave (Mamluk) Dynasty (1206-1290):</strong> Founded by **Qutb-ud-din Aibak** (started Qutub Minar, died playing Chaugan/polo). **Iltutmish** (real consolidator, introduced Silver *Tanka* & Copper *Jital* coins, created *Chahalgani* / group of 40 Turkish nobles, and *Iqta* system). **Raziya Sultan** (first and only female ruler). **Balban** (broke Chahalgani power, adopted policy of "Blood and Iron", introduced Persian festivals *Sajda* & *Paibos*).</li>
    <li><strong>Khilji Dynasty (1290-1320):</strong> Founded by Jalaluddin Khilji.
      <ul>
        <li>**Alauddin Khilji (1296-1316):** Greatest ruler. Military reforms: Introduced branding of horses (*Dag*) and descriptive rolls of soldiers (*Chehra*), paid soldiers in cash. **Market Reforms:** Set up controlled markets (*Mandi*, *Sarai-i-Adl*), appointed inspectors (*Shahna-i-Mandi*), fixed prices. Built Alai Darwaza.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Tughlaq Dynasty (1320-1414):</strong> Founded by Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq.
      <ul>
        <li>**Muhammad bin Tughlaq (1325-1351):** An intellectual but hasty ruler. **Failures:** Shifted capital from Delhi to Daulatabad (Deogiri); introduced token copper currency (led to mass counterfeiting); raised taxes in Doab region during famine; planned failed Qarachil and Khurasan military expeditions.</li>
        <li>**Firoz Shah Tughlaq (1351-1388):** Built canals for irrigation. Set up public welfare departments: *Diwan-i-Khairat* (charity), *Diwan-i-Bundagan* (slaves). Founded cities like Firozabad and Hissar.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Sayyid Dynasty (1414-1451):</strong> Founded by Khizr Khan.</li>
    <li><strong>Lodi Dynasty (1451-1526):</strong> Founded by Bahlul Lodi. Sikandar Lodi founded Agra (1504). **Ibrahim Lodi** was defeated by Babur in the **1st Battle of Panipat (1526)**, ending the Sultanate.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["vijayanagara-empire"] = `
  <h2>🏛️ The Vijayanagara Empire (1336 - 1646)</h2>
  <p>The supreme Hindu kingdom of Southern India, acting as a bulwark against northern Sultanate expansions.</p>

  <h3>1. History & Dynasties</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Founded in 1336 by brothers **Harihara I & Bukka I** (Sangama Dynasty) on the southern bank of Tungabhadra River, inspired by sage Vidyaranya.</li>
    <li>Four dynasties: Sangama, Saluva, Tuluva, and Aravidu.</li>
    <li>**Krishnadevaraya (Tuluva Dynasty: 1509 - 1529 CE):** The greatest ruler. Adorned court with *Ashtadiggajas* (eight Telugu poets, including Tenali Ramakrishna). Wrote *Amuktamalyada* (Telugu book on statecraft) and *Jambavati Kalyanam* (Sanskrit). Maintained friendly relations with Portuguese Governor Albuquerque. Built Vittala and Hazara Ramaswamy Temples at Hampi.</li>
    <li>**Battle of Talikota (1565):** Also known as Battle of Bannihatti. Combined forces of Deccan Sultanates (except Berar) defeated Vijayanagara army. Rama Raya was captured and executed. Capital Hampi was sacked.</li>
    <li>**Foreign Travellers:** Nicolo de Conti (Italy, 1420), Abdur Razzaq (Persia, 1443), Domingo Paes (Portugal, 1520), Fernao Nuniz (Portugal, 1535).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["bahmani-deccan-sultanates"] = `
  <h2>🏰 Bahmani Kingdom & Deccan Sultanates</h2>
  <p>The major Islamic state of Deccan, which continuously warred with Vijayanagara over the fertile Raichur Doab.</p>

  <h3>1. Bahmani Kingdom (1347 - 1527)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Founded in 1347 by **Ala-ud-Din Bahman Shah** (also known as Hasan Gangu) in revolt against Muhammad bin Tughlaq.</li>
    <li>Capital was initially Gulbarga, later shifted to Bidar. Mahmud Gawan (great prime minister) expanded power via administrative reforms.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Split into Deccan Sultanates</h3>
  <p>In the early 16th century, the Bahmani kingdom disintegrated into five independent sultanates:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Adil Shahis of Bijapur:</strong> Founded by Yusuf Adil Shah. Famous for building the **Gol Gumbaz** (largest dome in India, featuring a whispering gallery).</li>
    <li><strong>Nizam Shahis of Ahmadnagar:</strong> Malik Ahmad. Defended by Chand Bibi against Akbar's forces.</li>
    <li><strong>Qutb Shahis of Golconda:</strong> Quli Qutb Shah. Famous for Golconda Diamond mines. Built **Charminar** in Hyderabad.</li>
    <li><strong>Imad Shahis of Berar:</strong> Fathullah Imad-ul-Mulk.</li>
    <li><strong>Barid Shahis of Bidar:</strong> Qasim Barid.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["mughal-empire"] = `
  <h2>👑 The Mughal Empire (1526 - 1857)</h2>
  <p>Established by Central Asian invaders, the Mughals built an extensive, culturally rich empire in India.</p>

  <h3>1. Rulers & Triumphs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Babur (1526-1530):</strong> Defeated Ibrahim Lodi in **1st Battle of Panipat (1526)** using gunpowder and *Tulughma* (flanking) tactics. Defeated Rana Sanga in Battle of Khanwa (1527). Wrote autobiography *Tuzuk-i-Baburi* in Turkish.</li>
    <li><strong>Humayun (1530-1556):</strong> Defeated and exiled by Afghan leader **Sher Shah Suri** (who built Grand Trunk Road, introduced rupee currency, and Dahsala land reforms, ruling 1540-45). Humayun recaptured Delhi in 1555 but died falling from his library stairs.</li>
    <li><strong>Akbar (1556-1605):</strong> Crowned at 13. defeated Hemu in **2nd Battle of Panipat (1556)** with help of guardian Bairam Khan.
      <ul>
        <li>*Reforms:* Introduced **Mansabdari System** (grading officers by Zat/rank and Sawar/cavalry). **Todar Mal's Dahsala** (land revenue system based on 10-year average crop yields).</li>
        <li>*Religious Policy:* Abolished Jizya tax on non-Muslims (1564). Built **Ibadat Khana** (House of Worship) at Fatehpur Sikri. Promulgated **Din-i-Ilahi** (divine monotheism) in 1852.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Jahangir (1605-1627):</strong> Paintings peaked during his reign. Executed 5th Sikh Guru Arjan Dev. Captain Hawkins and Thomas Roe visited his court.</li>
    <li><strong>Shah Jahan (1628-1658):</strong> Golden Age of Architecture. Built Taj Mahal, Red Fort, Jama Masjid, Peacock Throne.</li>
    <li><strong>Aurangzeb (Alamgir: 1658-1707):</strong> Imprisoned his father Shah Jahan. Executed 9th Sikh Guru Tegh Bahadur. Re-imposed Jizya tax (1679). Annexed Bijapur (1686) and Golconda (1687). Called "Zinda Pir" (living saint) due to simple lifestyle.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["marathas"] = `
  <h2>⚔️ The Maratha Empire</h2>
  <p>The Marathas rose as a formidable power in the Deccan, challenging Mughal supremacy and eventually dominating North India.</p>

  <h3>1. Chhatrapati Shivaji Maharaj (1627 - 1680)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Born at Shivneri Fort. Captured major forts (Torna, Pratapgad, Purandar). Escaped Aurangzeb's house arrest in Agra (1666). Coronation at Raigad (1674), taking the title *Chhatrapati*.</li>
    <li><strong>Administration:</strong> **Ashtapradhan** (council of 8 ministers):
      <br/>- *Peshwa:* Prime Minister.
      <br/>- *Amatya:* Finance Minister.
      <br/>- *Senapati:* Military commander.
      <br/>- *Sumant / Dabir:* Foreign relations.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Revenue System:</strong>
      <br/>- *Chauth:* 1/4th (25%) of land revenue paid by neighboring states to escape Maratha raids.
      <br/>- *Sardeshmukhi:* Additional 10% tax claimed by Shivaji as supreme ruler.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Peshwa Era (1713 - 1818)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Peshwas became the de-facto rulers of the empire, shifting capital to Pune.
      <ul>
        <li>**Balaji Vishwanath (1713-1720):** Assisted Shahu in consolidating power.</li>
        <li>**Baji Rao I (1720-1740):** Undefeated in 41 battles (Palhed, Bhopal). Captured Salsette and Bassein from Portuguese. Expanded empire to Delhi.</li>
        <li>**Balaji Baji Rao (Nana Saheb: 1740-1761):** Maratha power peaked. **3rd Battle of Panipat (1761):** Maratha army under Sadashivrao Bhau was defeated by Afghan invader **Ahmad Shah Abdali**. This broke Maratha unity and halted expansion.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["sikh-history"] = `
  <h2>☬ Sikh History & Gurus</h2>
  <p>Sikhism evolved from a peaceful Bhakti movement to a highly militarized community in response to Mughal persecutions.</p>

  <h3>1. The Ten Gurus</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Guru Nanak Dev (1st):</strong> Founded Sikhism. Started **Langar** (free community kitchen) to break caste barriers.</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Angad (2nd):</strong> Standardized the **Gurmukhi script**.</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Ram Das (4th):</strong> Founded Amritsar on land gifted by Akbar.</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Arjan Dev (5th):</strong> Compiled the **Adi Granth**. Built the Golden Temple (Harmandir Sahib). Executed by **Jahangir**.</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Hargobind (6th):</strong> Built the **Akal Takht**. Militarized Sikhs, introduced *Miri* (temporal power) and *Piri* (spiritual power).</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Tegh Bahadur (9th):</strong> Executed by **Aurangzeb** at Delhi's Chandni Chowk for refusing to convert.</li>
    <li><strong>Guru Gobind Singh (10th):</strong> Founded the **Khalsa Panth** in 1699 at Anandpur Sahib. Introduced the 5 Ks. Declared **Guru Granth Sahib** as the permanent Guru.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Maharaja Ranjit Singh & British Wars</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Consolidated Punjab into a powerful empire. Signed the **Treaty of Amritsar (1809)** with Metcalfe/Minto, fixing Sutlej river as boundary.</li>
    <li>**Anglo-Sikh Wars:**
      <ul>
        <li><em>1st War (1845-46):</em> Ended with the **Treaty of Lahore** (Henry Hardinge GG).</li>
        <li><em>2nd War (1848-49):</em> Under **Lord Dalhousie**. Punjab was annexed to the British Empire (1849).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["bhakti-movement"] = `
  <h2>🙏 The Bhakti Movement</h2>
  <p>A medieval socio-religious reform movement preaching personal devotion to God as the path to salvation, bypassing priests and caste barriers.</p>

  <h3>1. Origins & Schools</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Originated in South India (7th-9th century) led by **Alvars** (Vaishnavite saints) and **Nayanars** (Shaivite saints).</li>
    <li>Spread to North India in the 14th century by **Ramananda**.</li>
    <li><strong>Saguna School (Worshipped God with Form):</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Mirabai:** Devoted to Krishna; composed Bhajans in Rajasthani/Braj.</li>
        <li>**Tulsidas:** Wrote *Ramcharitmanas* in Awadhi.</li>
        <li>**Chaitanya Mahaprabhu:** Bengal; popularized Kirtan (musical chanting).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li><strong>Nirguna School (Worshipped Formless God):</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Kabir:** Disciple of Ramananda. Opposed rituals, caste system, idol worship. Expressed ideas in *Dohas* (couplets) compiled in *Bijak*.</li>
        <li>**Guru Nanak:** Preached monotheism and equality.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["sufi-movement"] = `
  <h2>✨ The Sufi Movement</h2>
