window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["european-arrival"] = `
  <h2>🚢 European Entry and British Expansion</h2>
  <p>The search for spices led European powers to establish trading outposts in India, which eventually turned into territorial ambitions.</p>

  <h3>1. Arrival Sequence (PEDDF)</h3>
  <ol>
    <li>**Portuguese (1498):** Vasco da Gama landed at Calicut. Francisco de Almeida (Blue Water Policy). Alfonso de Albuquerque captured Goa (1510) from Bijapur, making it the capital. Introduced printing press (1556) and tobacco.</li>
    <li>**Dutch (1602):** First factory at Masulipatnam (1605). Displaced by British after the **Battle of Bedara (1759)**.</li>
    <li>**English (1600):** Queen Elizabeth granted charter. Hawkins (1608) and Roe (1615) secured trading rights from Jahangir. First factory at Surat (1613).</li>
    <li>**Danish (1616):** Settled at Tranquebar (TN) and Serampore (Bengal). Sold all holdings to British in 1845.</li>
    <li>**French (1664):** Founded by Colbert under Louis XIV. First factory at Surat (1667). François Martin founded Pondicherry (1674). Carnatic Wars (Anglo-French rivalry) crushed French power, culminating in their defeat at the **Battle of Wandiwash (1760)**.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>2. Foundation of British Rule</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Battle of Plassey (23 June 1757):</strong> Robert Clive defeated Nawab Siraj-ud-Daulah of Bengal. Clive bribed army chief **Mir Jafar**. Established EIC as a political force.</li>
    <li><strong>Battle of Buxar (22 Oct 1764):</strong> Hector Munro defeated joint forces of Nawab Mir Qasim of Bengal, Nawab Shuja-ud-Daulah of Awadh, and Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II. Led to **Treaty of Allahabad (1765)** where Clive secured **Diwani Rights** (tax collection rights) of Bengal, Bihar, and Odisha, starting the Dual Government.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["governor-generals-viceroys"] = `
  <h2>🎩 Governor-Generals & Viceroys</h2>
  <p>The administrative heads of British rule in India who designed expansionist and reformist policies.</p>

  <h3>1. Governor-Generals of Bengal (1773 - 1833)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Warren Hastings (1773-1785):</strong> First GG of Bengal. Ended Dual Government. Established civil/criminal courts. Impeached in London (later acquitted).</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Cornwallis (1786-1793):</strong> Father of Civil Services in India. Introduced **Permanent Settlement (Zamindari)** in Bengal (1793). Created Cornwallis Code (separating judiciary and executive).</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Wellesley (1798-1805):</strong> Introduced **Subsidiary Alliance System** (Hyderabad first to sign in 1798). Called himself "Bengal Tiger".</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Governor-Generals of India (1833 - 1858)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lord William Bentinck (1828-1835):</strong> First GG of India. Social Reforms: **Abolished Sati (1829, Regulation XVII)** with help of Raja Ram Mohan Roy. Suppressed Thuggee cult. Introduced English as medium of higher education (Macaulay's Minute 1835).</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Dalhousie (1848-1856):</strong> Great expansionist.
      <br/>- *Doctrine of Lapse:* Annexed Satara, Jhansi, Nagpur. Annexed Awadh (1856) on grounds of misgoverning.
      <br/>- *Infrastructural Reforms:* First railway line (Bombay-Thane 1853). First telegraph line (Calcutta-Agra). Postal system reforms. Wood's Dispatch on education (1854). Passed Hindu Widow Remarriage Act (1856).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Viceroys of India (1858 - 1947)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Lord Canning (1856-1862):</strong> First Viceroy. Suppressed 1857 Revolt. Ended East India Company rule (GoI Act 1858). Abolished Doctrine of Lapse. Introduced portfolio system.</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Lytton (1876-1880):</strong> Vernacular Press Act (1878 - censored local papers), Arms Act (1878). Grand Delhi Durbar (1877) amid famine.</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Ripon (1880-1884):</strong> Liberal Viceroy. Repealed Vernacular Press Act. Father of **Local Self-Government** in India. Passed first Factory Act (1881). Ilbert Bill Controversy (proposed allowing Indian judges to try Europeans). First synchronous census (1881).</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Curzon (1899-1905):</strong> Partitioned Bengal (1905) on communal lines, sparking Swadeshi movement. Established Archaeological Survey of India.</li>
    <li><strong>Lord Mountbatten (1947-1948):</strong> Last Viceroy. Planned partition (3rd June Plan). First GG of independent India.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["constitutional-development"] = `
  <h2>📜 Constitutional Development</h2>
  <p>The evolution of India's administrative and legal framework through British parliamentary interventions.</p>

  <h3>1. Company Regulation Acts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Regulating Act of 1773:</strong> First step by Parliament to supervise EIC. Governor of Bengal became GG of Bengal. Supreme Court at Calcutta set up.</li>
    <li><strong>Pitts India Act of 1784:</strong> Created a **Board of Control** in London to manage political affairs, establishing dual control.</li>
    <li><strong>Charter Act of 1813:</strong> Ended EIC's trade monopoly in India (except for tea and China trade). Allowed Christian missionaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Charter Act of 1833:</strong> Finished all commercial activities of EIC. GG of Bengal became **GG of India** (Bentinck).</li>
    <li><strong>Charter Act of 1853:</strong> Separated legislative and executive functions of GG's Council. Opened Civil Services to Indians.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Crown Administration Acts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Government of India Act 1858:</strong> Abolished EIC. Indian administration transferred to **British Crown**. Created Secretary of State for India.</li>
    <li><strong>Indian Councils Act 1909 (Morley-Minto Reforms):</strong> Introduced **Separate Electorate for Muslims** (legalized communalism). Satyendra Prasad Sinha joined Viceroy's Council.</li>
    <li><strong>Government of India Act 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms):</strong> Introduced **Dyarchy in Provinces** (Transferred/Reserved subjects). Bicameral legislature at Centre.</li>
    <li><strong>Government of India Act 1935:</strong> Proposed All-India Federation (never formed). Abolished dyarchy in provinces, introduced **Provincial Autonomy**. Set up Federal Court, RBI, and divided subjects into Federal, Provincial, and Concurrent lists.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["economic-impact-british"] = `
  <h2>💸 Economic Impact of British Rule</h2>
  <p>Colonial policies transformed India from a major exporter of finished goods into a supplier of raw materials and a market for British manufactures.</p>

  <h3>1. Three Land Revenue Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Permanent Settlement (Zamindari System):</strong> Lord Cornwallis (1793) in Bengal, Bihar, Odisha. Zamindars became owners of land. Tax rate fixed permanently; if not paid by sunset on due date, land was auctioned (**Sunset Law**). Extremely exploitative.</li>
    <li><strong>Ryotwari System:</strong> Thomas Munro and Alexander Reed (1820) in Madras, Bombay, Assam. Settlement made directly with the peasant (Ryot). Tax rate fixed at 50-60%, revised periodically.</li>
    <li><strong>Mahalwari System:</strong> Holt Mackenzie (1822) in Punjab, NW Province, Central India. Settlement made with the village community (Mahal). Individual peasants shared responsibility.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. High-Yield Economic Phenomena</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>De-industrialization:</strong> Heavy tariffs on Indian textiles in Britain, coupled with duty-free entry of British machine-made goods into India, destroyed India's famous handloom industries, leading to mass "ruralisation".</li>
    <li><strong>Drain of Wealth Theory:</strong> Pioneered by **Dadabhai Naoroji** in his book *Poverty and Un-British Rule in India* (1867). He demonstrated how Indian tax revenues and resources were sent to Britain (via home charges, military expenses, profits) without any equivalent material return, impoverishing India.</li>
    <li><strong>Commercialisation of Agriculture:</strong> Forced cultivation of cash crops (Indigo, Opium, Tea, Cotton) instead of foodgrains, leading to frequent, devastating famines (e.g., Great Bengal Famine of 1943).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["revolt-1857"] = `
  <h2>🔥 The Revolt of 1857</h2>
  <p>Often called India's First War of Independence. A massive armed uprising against East India Company rule.</p>

  <div style="text-align:center; margin: 15px 0;">
    <img src="images/history_1857_revolt_map.png" alt="Centers of the 1857 Revolt" style="max-width:100%; border-radius:8px; box-shadow:0 4px 10px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <p style="font-size:0.9em; color:#888; margin-top:5px;">Major Centers and Geographical Spread of the 1857 Uprising across Northern and Central India.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>1. Causes of the Revolt</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Political:</strong> Lord Dalhousie's Doctrine of Lapse and the annexation of Awadh (1856) on grounds of misgovernment alienated local rulers.</li>
    <li><strong>Economic:</strong> Heavy taxes on land and destruction of native handicrafts.</li>
    <li><strong>Social/Religious:</strong> Interference in social customs (abolishing Sati, Widow Remarriage Act) and fear of forced conversions by missionaries.</li>
    <li><strong>Military:</strong> Indian sepoys paid less than British, denied overseas allowance (*Batta*), and forced to travel across seas (violating caste rules).</li>
    <li><strong>Immediate:</strong> Introduction of the **Enfield Rifle** with grease paper cartridges containing pig and cow fat (offensive to Muslims and Hindus). **Mangal Pandey** of 34th NI shot his officer at Barrackpore (29 March 1857).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Major Centers & Leaders</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Center</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Indian Leader</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">British Officer suppressing</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Delhi</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Bahadur Shah II (nominal) / Bakht Khan (actual)</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">John Nicholson, Hudson</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Kanpur</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Nana Saheb, Tantia Tope (Ramachandra Pandurang)</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Colin Campbell</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Lucknow</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Begum Hazrat Mahal</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Colin Campbell, Havelock</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Jhansi</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Rani Laxmibai</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Hugh Rose (called her the only man among mutineers)</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Arrah (Bihar)</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kunwar Singh (80-year-old landlord)</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">William Taylor, Vincent Eyre</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>3. Outcomes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Passed the **Government of India Act 1858**, which transferred Indian administration from the East India Company to the **British Crown**.</li>
    <li>Governor-General's title changed to **Viceroy** (Canning was first).</li>
    <li>Abolished the Doctrine of Lapse. British stopped expanding territories, focusing on indirect control.</li>
    <li>**Army Reorganization:** Ratio of European to Indian soldiers increased. Regiments restructured on caste/religion lines ("Martial Races" theory) to prevent unity.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["socio-religious-reform"] = `
  <h2>🌱 Socio-Religious Reform Movements</h2>
  <p>Movements in the 19th century aiming to reform social evils like caste, Sati, child marriage, and promote modern education.</p>

  <h3>1. Reform Organizations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Brahmo Samaj (1828):</strong> Founded by **Raja Ram Mohan Roy** (Father of Indian Renaissance). Opposed idol worship, caste rigidity, and Sati (which was banned in 1829 due to his efforts). Promoted monotheism. Later split into Adi Brahmo Samaj (Debendranath Tagore) and Brahmo Samaj of India (Keshab Chandra Sen).</li>
    <li><strong>Arya Samaj (1875):</strong> Founded by **Swami Dayanand Saraswati** at Bombay. Motto: **"Go Back to Vedas"**. Wrote *Satyarth Prakash*. Opposed priesthood and idolatry. Started *Shuddhi* movement to reconvert people to Hinduism. Set up DAV schools.</li>
    <li><strong>Satyashodhak Samaj (1873):</strong> Founded by **Jyotirao Phule** in Maharashtra. Dedicated to the upliftment of lower castes and women. Wrote *Gulamgiri* (Slavery) and *Saraswati*. His wife Savitribai Phule started India's first school for girls.</li>
    <li><strong>Ramakrishna Mission (1897):</strong> Founded by **Swami Vivekananda** in honor of his Guru Ramakrishna Paramahansa. Vivekananda preached Practical Vedanta and humanitarian service. Gave the historic speech at Chicago's Parliament of Religions (1893).</li>
    <li><strong>Aligarh Movement (1875):</strong> Founded by **Sir Syed Ahmed Khan**. Opposed blind adherence to religion, promoted scientific education for Muslims. Founded Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College (now Aligarh Muslim University). Wrote *Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq*.</li>
    <li><strong>Prarthana Samaj (1867):</strong> Founded by Atmaram Pandurang in Bombay, later led by M.G. Ranade. Focused on inter-caste dining, widow remarriage, and women's education.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["freedom-movement"] = `
  <h2>⚔️ Indian National Movement</h2>
  <p>The organized struggle for independence from British rule, divided into Moderate, Extremist, and Gandhian phases.</p>

  <h3>1. Early Phases</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Indian National Congress (INC) Founded (1885):</strong> Founded by retired civil servant **A.O. Hume**. First session held at Bombay (W.C. Bonnerjee President, attended by 72 delegates).</li>
    <li><strong>Moderate Phase (1885-1905):</strong> Led by Naoroji, Gokhale, Banerjea. Believed in constitutional methods (Triple Ps: Prayers, Petitions, Protests).</li>
    <li><strong>Extremist Phase (1905-1919):</strong> Led by **Lal-Bal-Pal** (Lajpat Rai, Tilak, Pal) and Aurobindo Ghosh. Preached self-reliance, boycott, and passive resistance.
      <ul>
        <li>*Partition of Bengal (1905):* Announced by Lord Curzon. Triggered the **Swadeshi and Boycott Movement**.</li>
        <li>*Surat Split (1907):* Congress split into Moderates and Extremists over methods.</li>
        <li>*Lucknow Pact (1916):* Moderates/Extremists reunited. INC and Muslim League signed a joint pact.</li>
        <li>*Home Rule League (1916):* Tilak and Annie Besant started separate leagues demanding home rule.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Gandhian Era (1919 - 1947)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Gandhi returned from South Africa on **9 January 1915** (celebrated as Pravasi Bharatiya Divas).</li>
    <li><strong>Early Satyagrahas:</strong> Champaran (Bihar, 1917 - first civil disobedience against Indigo Tinkathia system), Ahmedabad Mill Strike (1918 - first hunger strike), Kheda (1918 - first non-cooperation). Mnemonic: **CAK** (Champaran, Ahmedabad, Kheda).</li>
    <li><strong>Rowlatt Act & Jallianwala Bagh (1919):</strong> Rowlatt Act allowed detention without trial. On 13 April 1919, General Dyer opened fire on a peaceful assembly at Jallianwala Bagh, Amritsar. Tagore renounced his Knighthood.</li>
    <li><strong>Non-Cooperation Movement (1920-1922):</strong> Launched alongside Khilafat movement. Called off by Gandhi in Feb 1922 after the **Chauri Chaura incident** (where a mob burned a police station, killing 22 policemen).</li>
    <li><strong>Civil Disobedience Movement (1930):</strong> Started with the **Dandi March** (Salt Satyagraha). Gandhi and 78 followers walked 240 miles from Sabarmati to Dandi (12 March - 6 April 1930) to break the salt monopoly.
      <br/>- *Round Table Conferences (1930-32):* Gandhi attended only the 2nd RTC following the Gandhi-Irwin Pact (1931).
      <br/>- *Poona Pact (1932):* Signed between Gandhi and B.R. Ambedkar, rejecting separate electorates for depressed classes, opting for reserved seats instead.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Quit India Movement (1942):</strong> Launched after the failure of the Cripps Mission. Gandhi gave the call **"Do or Die"** at Gowalia Tank, Bombay on 8 August 1942. Leaders were arrested overnight under *Operation Zero Hour*.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["post-independence-consolidation"] = `
  <h2>🇮🇳 Post-Independence Consolidation</h2>
  <p>The challenges of nation-building, border demarcations, princely states integration, and linguistic reorganization after 1947.</p>

  <h3>1. Integration of Princely States</h3>
  <p>Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel (Minister of States) and V.P. Menon successfully integrated 549 princely states. Three states presented exceptions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Junagadh:</strong> Nawab wanted to join Pakistan, but the Hindu majority population rebelled. Integrated via **Plebiscite (February 1948)**.</li>
    <li><strong>Hyderabad:</strong> Nizam refused to join India. Nizam's irregular militia (Razakars) terrorized the population. Integrated via military action code-named **Operation Polo (September 1948)**.</li>
    <li><strong>Jammu & Kashmir:</strong> Ruler Hari Singh wanted to remain independent. In October 1947, Pakistani armed tribal raiders invaded. Hari Singh signed the **Instrument of Accession (26 Oct 1947)**, and Indian troops flew in to defend Srinagar.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. States Reorganization</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Andhra Pradesh became the **first linguistic state** in 1953 following the death of Potti Sriramulu after a 56-day hunger strike.</li>
    <li>**Dhar Commission (1948)** and **JVP Committee (1948 - Jawaharlal, Vallabhbhai, Pattabhi)** rejected language as basis of states.</li>
    <li>Following Sriramulu's death, the government appointed the **Fazl Ali Commission (States Reorganization Commission, 1953)**. Its recommendations led to the **States Reorganization Act (1956)**, creating 14 states and 6 Union Territories.</li>
  </ul>
`;

// ==========================================
// 6. WORLD HISTORY
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["revolutions"] = `
  <h2>🇺🇸🇫🇷 Industrial & Political Revolutions</h2>
  <p>Key global revolutions that shaped modern political ideologies and economic structures.</p>

  <h3>1. American Revolution (1775 - 1783)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Causes:</strong> British taxation without representation (Stamp Act, Townshend Acts), trade restrictions.</li>
    <li><strong>Boston Tea Party (1773):</strong> Colonists dumped British tea into the harbor to protest tea tax.</li>
    <li><strong>Declaration of Independence:</strong> Drafted by Thomas Jefferson, adopted on **4 July 1776**.</li>
    <li><strong>Treaty of Paris (1783):</strong> Britain recognized the independence of the United States.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. French Revolution (1789)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Causes:</strong> Divison of French society into three estates (Clergy, Nobility, Commoners/Third Estate). Only the Third Estate paid taxes. Bankruptcy under Louis XVI.</li>
    <li><strong>Storming of the Bastille (14 July 1789):</strong> A fortress-prison, symbol of royal tyranny, was destroyed by a mob. Celebrated as France's National Day.</li>
    <li><strong>Motto:</strong> **"Liberty, Equality, Fraternity"**.</li>
    <li>Result: Executed Louis XVI and Marie Antoinette, ended feudalism, established republic, and paved the way for Napoleon Bonaparte's rise.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Russian Revolution (1917)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Overthrew the Tsarist autocracy. Caused by economic hardships of WWI, food shortages, and absolute rule of Tsar Nicholas II.</li>
    <li>**February Revolution:** Forced the Tsar to abdicate; provisional government set up.</li>
    <li>**October (Bolshevik) Revolution:** Led by **Vladimir Lenin**. Overthrew the provisional government, ending private property, establishing a socialist state (leading to the creation of the USSR in 1922).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>4. Industrial Revolution</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Began in Britain in the mid-18th century. Transitioned from hand production to machine manufacturing.</li>
    <li>Key inventions: James Watt's Steam Engine, Spinning Jenny, railway locomotives.</li>
    <li>Impact: Rise of capitalism, urbanization, growth of working class, and colonial search for raw materials.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["world-war-i"] = `
  <h2>💥 World War I (1914 - 1918)</h2>
  <p>A global war fought mainly in Europe, changing map boundaries and destroying empires.</p>

  <h3>1. Causes and Blocs</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Causes:</strong> Imperialist competition, secret alliances, arms race, militarism, and rise of nationalism.</li>
    <li><strong>Immediate Cause:</strong> Assassination of **Archduke Franz Ferdinand** (heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne) in Sarajevo (Bosnia) on 28 June 1914 by Serbian nationalist Gavrilo Princip.</li>
    <li><strong>Alliances:</strong>
      <br/>- *Allied Powers:* Britain, France, Russia, Italy, Japan, and USA (entered 1917 after German submarines sank US ships).
      <br/>- *Central Powers:* Germany, Austria-Hungary, Ottoman Empire (Turkey), Bulgaria.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Outcomes</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Ended with Germany's surrender on 11 Nov 1918. Signed the **Treaty of Versailles (1919)**, placing the guilt of war on Germany, stripping its colonies, and imposing massive reparations.</li>
    <li>Collpase of major empires: German, Austro-Hungarian, Russian (became USSR), and Ottoman (led to modern Turkey).</li>
    <li>Created the **League of Nations (1920)** to prevent future wars (failed due to US absence and lack of military power).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["world-war-ii"] = `
  <h2>💣 World War II (1939 - 1945)</h2>
  <p>The most destructive conflict in human history, involving the majority of the world's nations.</p>

  <h3>1. Causes and Outbreak</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Causes:</strong> Failure of the League of Nations, rise of fascism/nazism, policy of appeasement (Britain/France letting Hitler annex territory), and German dissatisfaction with Versailles.</li>
    <li><strong>Immediate Cause:</strong> Germany's invasion of **Poland** on 1 September 1939. Britain and France declared war on Germany.</li>
    <li><strong>Alliances:</strong>
      <br/>- *Axis Powers:* Germany (Hitler), Italy (Mussolini), Japan (Tojo).
      <br/>- *Allied Powers:* Britain (Churchill), USSR (Stalin), USA (Roosevelt / Truman), China.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Decisive Events & End</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>**Pearl Harbor (7 Dec 1941):** Japan attacked US naval base in Hawaii. Prompted USA to enter the war.</li>
    <li>**Battle of Stalingrad (1942-43):** Turning point in Europe. Soviet Red Army defeated German forces.</li>
    <li>**D-Day (6 June 1944):** Allied invasion of Normandy (France), liberating Western Europe.</li>
    <li>**The Holocaust:** Systematic, state-sponsored genocide of 6 million Jews by Nazi Germany.</li>
    <li>**End:** Germany surrendered in May 1945 (Hitler committed suicide). Japan surrendered after USA dropped atomic bombs: **"Little Boy"** on **Hiroshima** (6 August 1945) and **"Fat Man"** on **Nagasaki** (9 August 1945).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["interwar-period"] = `
  <h2>📉 The Interwar Period (1919 - 1939)</h2>
  <p>The twenty years between the two World Wars, marked by economic collapse and the rise of totalitarian dictators.</p>

  <h3>1. Major Developments</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>League of Nations:</strong> Established in 1920 to maintain peace. Proved weak; could not halt invasions (e.g., Japan invading Manchuria 1931; Italy invading Ethiopia 1935).</li>
    <li><strong>Great Depression (1929):</strong> Began with the Wall Street stock market crash in USA. Led to worldwide decline in trade, high unemployment, and financial collapse, weakening democratic governments.</li>
    <li><strong>Rise of Dictators:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**Fascism in Italy:** Benito Mussolini seized power in 1922. Promoted aggressive nationalism and corporate state.</li>
        <li>**Nazism in Germany:** Adolf Hitler (Nazi Party) became Chancellor in 1933. Preached racial superiority, anti-Semitism, and rejected the Treaty of Versailles.</li>
        <li>**Militarism in Japan:** Military leaders took control of government, expanding into China.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["cold-war"] = `
  <h2>❄️ The Cold War & Decolonisation</h2>
  <p>An era of ideological, military, and political tension between two superpowers (USA and USSR), coupled with the liberation of colonies in Asia and Africa.</p>

  <h3>1. Ideological Rivalry</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>**USA (Western Bloc):** Capitalist, democratic.</li>
    <li>**USSR (Eastern Bloc):** Communist, authoritarian.</li>
    <li>**Military Blocs:** NATO (1949, led by USA) vs. Warsaw Pact (1955, led by USSR).</li>
    <li>**Proxy Wars:** Fought indirectly in Korea (1950-53) and Vietnam (1955-75).</li>
    <li>**Key Confrontations:**
      <ul>
        <li>*Berlin Blockade (1948):* Soviets blocked West Berlin. Allied airlift broke the blockade. Berlin Wall built in 1961.</li>
        <li>*Cuban Missile Crisis (1962):* Closest the world came to nuclear war. USSR placed missiles in Cuba; USA imposed naval blockade. Resolved when USSR withdrew missiles.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
    <li>**Collapse:** Berlin Wall fell in 1989. The **Soviet Union (USSR) disintegrated** in December 1991, ending the Cold War.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Decolonisation</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>The decline of European economic power post-WWII led to independence movements across Asia and Africa. India (1947), Indonesia (1949), Ghana (1957), Algeria (1962).</li>
    <li>**Non-Aligned Movement (NAM):** Formed in Belgrade (1961) by leaders like Nehru (India), Nasser (Egypt), Tito (Yugoslavia), Sukarno (Indonesia), and Nkrumah (Ghana) to remain neutral and avoid joining either US or Soviet blocs.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["international-institutions"] = `
  <h2>🇺🇳 International Institutions</h2>
  <p>Global bodies created to manage international relations, trade, finance, and security.</p>

  <h3>1. The United Nations (UN)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Established on **24 October 1945** (UN Day) to replace the League of Nations. Headquarters in New York.</li>
    <li><strong>Six Principal Organs:</strong>
      <ul>
        <li>**General Assembly:** Deciding body where all member states have equal votes.</li>
        <li>**Security Council:** Responsible for international peace. Has 15 members: 5 Permanent (P5 with veto power: USA, UK, France, Russia, China) and 10 non-permanent members elected for 2-year terms.</li>
        <li>**International Court of Justice (ICJ):** Judicial body. Located at **The Hague, Netherlands**.</li>
        <li>**Secretariat:** Executive branch, led by the Secretary-General.</li>
        <li>Economic and Social Council (ECOSOC) and Trusteeship Council (suspended).</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Financial & Trade Bodies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Bretton Woods Twins (1944):</strong> Created at the Bretton Woods Conference:
      <br/>- *International Monetary Fund (IMF):* Manages balance of payments and exchange rates.
      <br/>- *World Bank (IBRD):* Provides long-term development loans.
    </li>
    <li><strong>World Trade Organization (WTO):</strong> Established on **1 January 1995**, replacing the General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade (GATT). Regulates global trade.</li>
  </ul>
`;

// ==========================================
// 7. ART, CULTURE & HERITAGE
// ==========================================

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["architecture"] = `
  <h2>🏛️ Indian Architecture</h2>
  <p>The structural heritage of India, from ancient cave temples to medieval Islamic monuments.</p>

  <h3>1. Temple Architecture Styles</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Nagara Style (North India):</strong> Platform base, curved tower (**Shikhara**), assembly hall (*Mandapa*), no boundary walls. E.g., Kandariya Mahadeva (Khajuraho), Sun Temple (Konark).</li>
    <li><strong>Dravida Style (South India):</strong> Monumental gateway (**Gopuram**), stepped pyramidal tower (**Vimana**), temple tanks, high boundary walls. E.g., Brihadeshwara Temple (Thanjavur).</li>
    <li><strong>Vesara Style (Deccan):</strong> Hybrid of Nagara and Dravida, characterized by star-shaped platform (*Jagati*). E.g., Chennakeshava Temple (Belur).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Indo-Islamic & Mughal Architecture</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Introduced the arch, dome, minaret, mortar, and geometric designs (avoiding human figures).</li>
    <li>**Delhi Sultanate:** Qutub Minar (Aibak/Iltutmish), Alai Darwaza (Alauddin Khilji), Tomb of Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq.</li>
    <li>**Mughal Architecture:** 
      <br/>- *Akbar:* Red sandstone structures. Humayun's Tomb (first double-dome), Fatehpur Sikri (Buland Darwaza, Panch Mahal).
      <br/>- *Shah Jahan:* White marble structures. Taj Mahal, Red Fort (Delhi), Moti Masjid (Agra). Pietra Dura (stone inlay work) reached peak.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>3. Stupas, Caves & Monuments</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>**Sanchi Stupa (MP):** Commemorated by Ashoka. Relics of Buddha inside semicircular mound with ornamental gateways (*Toranas*).</li>
    <li>**Ajanta Caves:** 29 Buddhist rock-cut caves. Murals depicting Jataka tales.</li>
    <li>**Ellora Caves:** 34 caves representing Hinduism, Buddhism, and Jainism. Cave 16 contains the monolithic rock-cut **Kailash Temple** built by Krishna I (Rashtrakuta).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["paintings"] = `
  <h2>🎨 Painting Traditions of India</h2>
  <p>From ancient cave murals to royal miniature schools and tribal folk paintings.</p>

  <h3>1. Murals & Miniatures</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Mural Paintings:</strong> Giant paintings done on walls/rocks. Prominent at Ajanta Caves (Jataka tales, Fresco technique - painting on wet plaster) and Bagh.</li>
    <li><strong>Miniature Paintings:</strong> Small, detailed paintings on paper or palm leaves.
      <br/>- *Mughal School:* Introduced court scenes, portraits, hunting scenes. Reached peak under **Jahangir**.
      <br/>- *Rajput School:* Focused on religious themes (Radha-Krishna, Ramayana) and courtly lives. E.g., Kangra, Basohli, Kishangarh (famous painting *Bani Thani*).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Folk Paintings</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Madhubani Paintings (Bihar):</strong> Mithila region. Traditionally done by women using rice paste, twigs, fingers, and natural dyes. Displays nature, birds, fish, and deities. Has geographical indication (GI) tag.</li>
    <li><strong>Warli Paintings (Maharashtra):</strong> Tribal art using basic geometric shapes (triangles, circles, squares) made of white paste (rice flour) on mud/ochre walls, showing daily activities, crop cycles, and tarpa dance.</li>
    <li><strong>Pattachitra (Odisha):</strong> Scroll paintings on cloth or dried palm leaves. Displays tales of Lord Jagannath and Krishna.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["dance-music"] = `
  <h2>💃 Dance & Music</h2>
  <p>The classical and folk performance heritage of India, preserving historical myths and regional identities.</p>

  <h3>1. Eight Classical Dances</h3>
  <table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin-top:12px; font-size:0.85rem;">
    <tr style="background-color: rgba(255,255,255,0.15); font-weight: bold;">
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Dance</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">State</th>
      <th style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Key Characteristics</th>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Bharatnatyam</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Tamil Nadu</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Oldest classical dance. Devadasi origin. Solo performance, fire dance elements.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Kathak</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Uttar Pradesh</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Storytelling, fast spins (*chakkars*), complex footwork. Nawab Wajid Ali Shah.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Kathakali</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kerala</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Dance-drama. Complex face makeup (Green: virtue; Red/Black: evil).</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Mohiniyattam</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Kerala</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Solo feminine dance. Story of Mohini (enchantress). White/Gold saree.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Kuchipudi</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Andhra Pradesh</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Tarangam (dancing on brass plates). Dancers sing and act.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Odissi</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Odisha</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">**Tribhanga posture** (three bends of body). Worshipped in temples.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Manipuri</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Manipur</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Vaishnavite themes (Raslila). Cylindrical skirt. Soft, flowing movements.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>Sattriya</strong></td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Assam</td>
      <td style="padding:8px; border:1px solid var(--border);">Introduced by **Srimanta Sankardev** in Vaishnavite monasteries.</td>
    </tr>
  </table>

  <h3>2. Classical Music Systems</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hindustani Music (North India):</strong> Heavy Persian/Central Asian influence. Divided into *Gharanas* (schools). Major styles: *Dhrupad* (dhruva/fixed, older), *Khayal* (imagination, popular).</li>
    <li><strong>Carnatic Music (South India):</strong> Purely indigenous system. Structured format based on *Kriti* (composition), *Raga* (melody), and *Tala* (rhythm). Trinity: Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar, Syama Sastri.</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["literature"] = `
  <h2>📖 Literary Traditions of India</h2>
  <p>The vast text corpus of India, preserving scientific, political, and philosophical achievements.</p>

  <h3>1. Ancient Scripts & Texts</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Vedas:</strong> Rigveda (1028 hymns), Samaveda (music), Yajurveda (prose/ritual instructions), Atharvaveda (medicine/spells).</li>
    <li><strong>Epics:</strong> Ramayana (Valmiki) and Mahabharata (Vyasa).</li>
    <li><strong>Buddhist Canons (Tripitakas - in Pali):</strong> Sutta Pitaka (Buddha's discourses), Vinaya Pitaka (rules for monks), Abhidhamma Pitaka (philosophical analysis).</li>
    <li><strong>Jain Canons:</strong> Angas (written in Prakrit/Ardhamagadhi).</li>
    <li><strong>Sanskrit Plays/Treatises:</strong>
      <br/>- *Ashtadhyayi (Panini):* First scientific Sanskrit grammar.
      <br/>- *Mahabhashya (Patanjali):* Commentary on Panini.
      <br/>- *Abhijnanasakuntalam (Kalidasa):* Masterpiece play of Gupta era.
      <br/>- *Mrichchakatikam (Shudraka):* "The Little Clay Cart" - social drama of ancient times.
      <br/>- *Mudrarakshasa (Vishakhadatta):* Historical play showing Chandragupta Maurya's rise.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Sangam Literature (Tamil):</strong> Tolkappiyam (grammar), epics Silappatikaram and Manimekalai, and Tirukkural (moral guide by Tiruvalluvar).</li>
  </ul>
`;

window.EXPANDED_NOTES_DATA["religion-festivals"] = `
  <h2>🕉️ Indian Philosophy & Festivals</h2>
  <p>The intellectual schools of thought and cultural celebrations across communities in India.</p>

  <h3>1. Six Orthodox Schools (Shad-Darshanas)</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Samkhya (Sage Kapila):</strong> Oldest school. Dualism of Prakriti (matter) and Purusha (soul).</li>
    <li><strong>Yoga (Sage Patanjali):</strong> Focuses on physical and mental control as path to liberation.</li>
    <li><strong>Nyaya (Sage Gautama):</strong> Logicians. Stresses logical thinking and verification.</li>
    <li><strong>Vaisheshika (Sage Kanada):</strong> Introduced atom theory (*Anu*). Proved universe is made of physical elements.</li>
    <li><strong>Mimamsa (Sage Jaimini):</strong> Stresses the ritualistic teachings of the Vedas.</li>
    <li><strong>Vedanta (Sage Badarayana):</strong> Explores philosophical essence of Upanishads. Later divided into Advaita (monism of Adi Shankara) and Vishishtadvaita (Ramanuja).</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>2. Regional Festivals</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>**Onam (Kerala):** Harvest festival, celebrates King Mahabali's return. Famous for Snake Boat Races (*Vallam Kali*).</li>
    <li>**Pongal (Tamil Nadu):** 4-day harvest festival.</li>
    <li>**Bihu (Assam):** 3 festivals (Rongali, Kongali, Bhogali) marking agricultural cycles.</li>
    <li>**Hornbill Festival (Nagaland):** "Festival of Festivals", showcasing rich tribal heritage.</li>
  </ul>
`;

